---
title: "Add corpus entries for a CAP"
description: "Register an entry ID, produce the bytes from a local stellar-core, a golden, network history or a recipe, write what a correct reader must derive, and prove it with verify."
---

For SDK maintainers, indexer authors and protocol engineers who need a shape the corpus lacks, such as the new
arms of a CAP or a rare value seen on mainnet. After this page you can add a corpus entry: bytes stellar-core
emitted, tagged with the CAP and protocol, with the values a correct reader must produce, checked against
rs-stellar-xdr and stellar-core's goldens. Every SDK and indexer that runs the corpus is then tested against it.

## Prerequisites

- Linux on x86-64 with cgroup v2 and unprivileged user namespaces (for `OFFLINE=1`), Docker, and about 15 GB of
  disk. See the [requirements](/quickstart#requirements).
- The full fetch, which includes stellar-core, its goldens and every dependency:

  ```sh
  make fetch && make build
  ```

## Steps

<Steps>
  <Step title="Register the ID">

    An entry ID is born in `corpus/plan.json` and nowhere else. Search the plan first: the pattern may already exist
    under another name. If it is new, add a row with every key the plan schema (`corpus/schema/plan.schema.json`)
    requires: kind, CAPs, minimum protocol, source, priority, what it serves. Run `make repo-checks`, and send the
    row as its own change.

    One entry is one pattern. The same pattern on another network or protocol is a variant of the same entry.

  </Step>
  <Step title="Produce the bytes">

    Pick the source in this order, and never edit bytes:

    <Tabs>
      <Tab title="Local stellar-core">

        Write a scenario under `corpus/scenarios/` (schema `corpus/schema/scenario.schema.json`) and run it on
        both passphrases:

        ```sh
        xdrcheck-corpus gen corpus/scenarios/<your-scenario>.yaml           # testnet and pubnet passphrases by default
        xdrcheck-corpus gen --check corpus/scenarios/<your-scenario>.yaml   # regenerate and compare
        ```

        The generator runs a standalone, offline stellar-core with manual close. Anyone can reproduce the bytes.
        Scenario placeholders (`${acct.L}`, `${muxed(L, id)}`, `${signer.payload(L, hex)}`, …) and setup items are
        listed in [Writing a scenario](#5-writing-a-scenario-local-core-entries).

      </Tab>
      <Tab title="stellar-core golden">

        ```sh
        xdrcheck-corpus fetch-goldens <tag>
        xdrcheck-corpus import-golden <tag> "<test name>" --entry <entry id>
        ```

        Goldens are named by the human test name, since golden file names change with every protocol.

      </Tab>
      <Tab title="Network history">

        ```sh
        xdrcheck-corpus capture testnet <ledger> --tx <hash> --entry <entry id>
        ```

        Fetches one object from the public SEP-54 data lake verbatim and records its provenance.

      </Tab>
      <Tab title="Synthetic">

        Only when stellar-core cannot or would not emit the bytes. Write a recipe (schema `recipe.schema.json`)
        and build it with `xdrcheck-corpus synth <recipe>`. The entry is marked `synthetic: true` with the reason.

      </Tab>
    </Tabs>

  </Step>
  <Step title="Render the decoded value">

    ```sh
    xdrcheck-corpus render <entry id>
    ```

    Writes the SEP-51 decoded sidecar and fills the derived fields (`requires`, `derived.l1`).

  </Step>
  <Step title="Write expect">

    State what a correct reader must derive, from two things only: the scenario's intent and the decoded bytes.
    Never take an expected value from the output of an SDK or indexer under test.

    | Block | Holds |
    |---|---|
    | `expect.ledger` | counts of transactions, operations, events and entry changes for the whole ledger |
    | `expect.transactions` | every transaction with its hash and `successful` |
    | `expect.values` | a few pinpoints into the decoded value, for the fields the pattern is about |
    | `participants` | `must_include` base addresses; `must_not_include` the addresses a careless reader would add, each with `because` |
    | `state_changes`, `movements` | one item per reader-visible change or value movement |

    Set `validity` right after `synthetic`: `valid` for anything stellar-core wrote. See [Entries](/architecture/corpus#entries).

  </Step>
  <Step title="Compare with the reference extractor">

    ```sh
    xdrcheck-corpus extract <entry id>
    ```

    It derives participants, state changes and movements from ledger-entry changes and events only. Resolve every
    disagreement by reading the bytes again, not by copying either side.

  </Step>
  <Step title="Index and verify">

    ```sh
    xdrcheck-corpus index
    make repo-checks
    make verify-corpus OFFLINE=1
    make verify-coverage OFFLINE=1
    ```

    `verify-corpus` checks that the entry round-trips through rs-stellar-xdr, that its sidecar and pinpoints agree,
    that the reference extractor agrees with `expect`, and that golden entries equal stellar-core's goldens.
    `verify-coverage` checks that the coverage report lists the new entry under its CAP.

  </Step>
</Steps>

## What success looks like

Every command above exits 0, and the entry appears under its CAP in `corpus/COVERAGE.md`. Before you send the
change, answer each question of the [checklist](#6-checklist-before-you-send-an-entry) for every entry.

## Troubleshooting

<AccordionGroup>
  <Accordion title="verify says a value cannot be derived">

    A value you stated that the extractor cannot derive from the bytes fails `verify`. Correct it, or keep it with
    `"unverified": "<reason>"` on the item.

  </Accordion>
  <Accordion title="gen --check reports different bytes">

    Generation must be reproducible: the same ledger header and transaction hashes, and the same bytes after
    stellar-core's golden normalisation. A scenario that depends on wall-clock time or unordered input breaks this.

  </Accordion>
  <Accordion title="A protocol is above what the build supports">

    The oracle refuses a ledger above `max_protocol`. Shapes of the next protocol go into the `-next` channel; see
    [Maintain the kit through a protocol upgrade](/guides/maintain-kit).

  </Accordion>
</AccordionGroup>

